\id MRK Autogenerated BSB by bsb2usfm

\usfm 3.1
\h Mark
\toc1 The Gospel According to Mark
\toc2 Mark
\toc3 Mrk
\mt2 The Gospel According to
\mt1 Mark
\c 1
\s1 The Mission of John the Baptist
\r (\ref Isaiah 40:1–5|ISA 40:1-5\ref*; \ref Matthew 3:1–17|MAT 3:1-17\ref*; \ref Luke 3:1–22|LUK 3:1-22\ref*; \ref John 1:19–34|JHN 1:19-34\ref*)
\p \v 1 This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.\f + \fr 1:1 \ft ECM, NE, BYZ, and TR; SBL and WH \fqa the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ.\f* \v 2 As it is written in Isaiah the prophet:\f + \fr 1:2 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa in the prophets:\f*
\b 
\q1 “Behold, I will send My messenger ahead of You,
\q2 who will prepare Your way.”\f + \fr 1:2 \ft \ref Malachi 3:1|MAL 3:1\ref*\f*
\q1 \v 3 “A voice of one calling in the wilderness,
\q2 ‘Prepare the way for the Lord,
\q2 make straight paths for Him.’”\f + \fr 1:3 \ft \ref Isaiah 40:3|ISA 40:3\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p \v 4 John the Baptist appeared in the wilderness, preaching a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins. \v 5 People went out to him from all of Jerusalem and the countryside of Judea. Confessing their sins, they were baptized by him in the Jordan River.
\p \v 6 John was clothed in camel’s hair, with a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey. \v 7 And he proclaimed: “After me will come One more powerful than I, the straps of whose sandals I am not worthy to stoop down and untie. \v 8 I baptize you with water,\f + \fr 1:8 \ft Or \fqa in water\f* but He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit.\f + \fr 1:8 \ft Or \fqa in the Holy Spirit\f*”
\p \v 9 In those days Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee and was baptized by John in the Jordan. \v 10 As soon as Jesus came up out of the water, He saw\f + \fr 1:10 \ft Or \fqa he saw\ft ; see \ref John 1:32–33|JHN 1:32-33\ref*\f* the heavens breaking open and the Spirit descending on Him like a dove. \v 11 And a voice came from heaven: “You are My beloved Son; in You I am well pleased.”
\s1 The Temptation and Preaching of Jesus
\r (\ref Matthew 4:1–17|MAT 4:1-17\ref*; \ref Luke 4:1–15|LUK 4:1-15\ref*)
\p \v 12 At once the Spirit drove Jesus into the wilderness, \v 13 and He was there for forty days, being tempted by Satan. He was with the wild animals, and the angels ministered to Him.
\p \v 14 After the arrest of John, Jesus went into Galilee and proclaimed the gospel of God.\f + \fr 1:14 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa the gospel of the kingdom of God \f*\wj \v 15 “The time is fulfilled,”\wj* He said, \wj “and the kingdom of God is near. Repent and believe in the gospel!”\wj*
\s1 The First Disciples
\r (\ref Matthew 4:18–22|MAT 4:18-22\ref*; \ref Luke 5:1–11|LUK 5:1-11\ref*; \ref John 1:35–42|JHN 1:35-42\ref*)
\p \v 16 As Jesus was walking beside the Sea of Galilee, He saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the sea, for they were fishermen. \wj \v 17 “Come, follow Me,”\wj* Jesus said, \wj “and I will make you fishers of men.”\wj* \v 18 And at once they left their nets and followed Him.
\p \v 19 Going on a little farther, He saw James son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat, mending their nets. \v 20 Immediately Jesus called them, and they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men and followed Him.
\s1 Jesus Expels an Unclean Spirit
\r (\ref Luke 4:31–37|LUK 4:31-37\ref*)
\p \v 21 Then Jesus and His companions went to Capernaum, and right away Jesus entered the synagogue on the Sabbath and began to teach. \v 22 The people were astonished at His teaching, because He taught as one who had authority, and not as the scribes.
\p \v 23 Suddenly a man with an unclean spirit cried out in the synagogue: \v 24 “What do You  want with us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have You come to destroy us? I know who You are — the Holy One of God!”
\p \v 25 But Jesus rebuked the spirit. \wj “Be silent!”\wj* He said. \wj “Come out of him!”\wj* \v 26 At this, the unclean spirit threw the man into convulsions and came out with a loud shriek.
\p \v 27 All the people were amazed and began to ask one another, “What is this? A new teaching with authority! He commands even the unclean spirits, and they obey Him!” \v 28 And the news about Jesus spread quickly through the whole region of Galilee.
\s1 Jesus Heals at Peter’s House
\r (\ref Matthew 8:14–17|MAT 8:14-17\ref*; \ref Luke 4:38–41|LUK 4:38-41\ref*)
\p \v 29 As soon as Jesus and His companions had left the synagogue, they went with James and John to the home of Simon and Andrew. \v 30 Simon’s mother-in-law was sick in bed with a fever, and they promptly told Jesus about her. \v 31 So He went to her, took her by the hand, and helped her up. The fever left her, and she began to serve them.
\p \v 32 That evening, after sunset, people brought to Jesus all the sick and demon-possessed, \v 33 and the whole town gathered at the door. \v 34 And He healed many who were ill with various diseases and drove out many demons. But He would not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who He was.
\s1 Jesus Prays and Preaches
\r (\ref Luke 4:42–44|LUK 4:42-44\ref*)
\p \v 35 Early in the morning, while it was still dark, Jesus got up and went out to a solitary place to pray. \v 36 Simon and his companions went to look for Him, \v 37 and when they found Him, they said, “Everyone is looking for You!”
\p \v 38 But Jesus answered, \wj “Let us go on to the neighboring towns so I can preach there as well, for that is why I have come.”\wj* \v 39 So He went throughout Galilee, preaching in their synagogues and driving out demons.
\s1 The Leper’s Prayer
\r (\ref Leviticus 14:1–32|LEV 14:1-32\ref*; \ref Matthew 8:1–4|MAT 8:1-4\ref*; \ref Luke 5:12–16|LUK 5:12-16\ref*)
\p \v 40 Then a leper\f + \fr 1:40 \ft A \fqa leper \ft was one afflicted with a skin disease. See Leviticus 13.\f* came to Jesus, begging on his knees: “If You are willing, You can make me clean.”
\p \v 41 Moved with compassion,\f + \fr 1:41 \ft SBL \fqa Moved with indignation\f* Jesus reached out His hand and touched the man. \wj “I am willing,”\wj* He said. \wj “Be clean!”\wj* \v 42 And immediately the leprosy left him, and the man was cleansed.
\p \v 43 Jesus promptly sent him away with a stern warning: \wj \v 44 “See that you don’t tell anyone. But go, show yourself to the priest and present the offering Moses prescribed for your cleansing, as a testimony to them.”\f + \fr 1:44 \ft See \ref Leviticus 14:1–32|LEV 14:1-32\ref*.\f*\wj*
\p \v 45 But the man went out and openly began to proclaim and spread the news.
\p Consequently, Jesus could no longer enter a town in plain view, but He stayed out in solitary places. Yet people came to Him from every quarter.
\c 5
\s1 The Demons and the Pigs
\r (\ref Matthew 8:28–34|MAT 8:28-34\ref*; \ref Luke 8:26–39|LUK 8:26-39\ref*)
\p \v 1 On the other side of the sea, they arrived in the region of the Gerasenes.\f + \fr 5:1 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa Gadarenes\ft ; PT \fqa Gergesenes\f* \v 2 As soon as Jesus got out of the boat, He was met by a man with an unclean spirit, who was coming from the tombs. \v 3 This man had been living in the tombs and could no longer be restrained, even with chains. \v 4 Though he was often bound with chains and shackles, he had broken the chains and shattered the shackles. Now there was no one with the strength to subdue him. \v 5 Night and day in the tombs and in the mountains he kept crying out and cutting himself with stones.
\p \v 6 When the man saw Jesus from a distance, he ran and fell on his knees before Him. \v 7 And he shouted in a loud voice, “What do You want with me, Jesus, Son of the Most High God? I beg You before God not to torture me!” \v 8 For Jesus had already declared, \wj “Come out of this man, you unclean spirit!”\wj*
\p \wj \v 9 “What is your name?”\wj* Jesus asked.
\p “My name is Legion,” he replied, “for we are many.” \v 10 And he begged Jesus repeatedly not to send them out of that region.
\p \v 11 There on the nearby hillside a large herd of pigs was feeding. \v 12 So the demons begged Jesus, “Send us to the pigs, so that we may enter them.”
\p \v 13 He gave them permission, and the unclean spirits came out and went into the pigs, and the herd of about two thousand rushed down the steep bank into the sea and drowned in the water.\f + \fr 5:13 \ft Literally \fqa and were drowned in the sea\f*
\p \v 14 Those tending the pigs ran off and reported this in the town and countryside, and the people went out to see what had happened. \v 15 When they came to Jesus, they saw the man who had been possessed by the legion of demons sitting there, clothed and in his right mind; and they were afraid.
\p \v 16 Those who had seen it described what had happened to the demon-possessed man and also to the pigs. \v 17 And the people began to beg Jesus to leave their region.
\p \v 18 As He was getting into the boat, the man who had been possessed by the demons begged to go with Him. \v 19 But Jesus would not allow him. \wj “Go home to your own people,”\wj* He said, \wj “and tell them how much the Lord has done for you, and what mercy He has shown you.”\wj*
\p \v 20 So the man went away and began to proclaim throughout the Decapolis\f + \fr 5:20 \ft That is, the Ten Cities\f* how much Jesus had done for him. And everyone was amazed.
\s1 The Healing Touch of Jesus
\r (\ref Matthew 9:18–26|MAT 9:18-26\ref*; \ref Luke 8:40–56|LUK 8:40-56\ref*)
\p \v 21 When Jesus had again crossed by boat to the other side, a large crowd gathered around Him beside the sea. \v 22 A synagogue leader named Jairus arrived, and seeing Jesus, he fell at His feet \v 23 and pleaded with Him urgently, “My little daughter is near death. Please come and place Your hands on her, so that she will be healed and live.”
\p \v 24 So Jesus went with him, and a large crowd followed and pressed around Him. \v 25 And a woman was there who had suffered from bleeding for twelve years. \v 26 She had borne much agony under the care of many physicians and had spent all she had, but to no avail. Instead, her condition had only grown worse.
\p \v 27 When the woman heard about Jesus, she came up through the crowd behind Him and touched His cloak. \v 28 For she kept saying, “If only I touch His garments, I will be healed.” \v 29 Immediately her bleeding stopped, and she sensed in her body that she was healed of her affliction.
\p \v 30 At once Jesus was aware that power had gone out from Him. Turning to the crowd, He asked, \wj “Who touched My garments?”\wj*
\p \v 31 His disciples answered, “You can see the crowd pressing in on You, and yet You ask, \wj ‘Who touched Me?’\wj*”
\p \v 32 But He kept looking around to see who had done this. \v 33 Then the woman, knowing what had happened to her, came and fell down before Him trembling in fear, and she told Him the whole truth.
\p \wj \v 34 “Daughter,”\wj* said Jesus, \wj “your faith has healed you. Go in peace and be free of your affliction.”\wj*
\p \v 35 While He was still speaking,  messengers  from the house of Jairus arrived and said, “Your daughter is dead; why bother the Teacher anymore?”
\p \v 36 But Jesus overheard\f + \fr 5:36 \ft Or \fqa ignored\f* their conversation and said to Jairus, \wj “Do not be afraid; just believe.”\wj* \v 37 And He did not allow anyone to accompany Him except Peter, James, and John the brother of James.
\p \v 38 When they arrived at the house of the synagogue leader, Jesus saw the commotion and the people weeping and wailing loudly. \v 39 He went inside and asked, \wj “Why all this commotion and weeping? The child is not dead, but asleep.”\wj* \v 40 And they laughed at Him.
\p After He had put them all outside, He took the child’s father and mother and His own companions, and went in to see the child. \v 41 Taking her by the hand, Jesus said, \wj “Talitha koum!”\wj* which means, \wj “Little girl, I say to you, get up!”\wj* \v 42 Immediately the girl got up and began to walk around (she was twelve years old). And at once they were utterly astounded. \v 43 Then Jesus gave strict orders that no one should know about this, and He told them to give her something to eat.
\c 6
\s1 The Rejection at Nazareth
\r (\ref Matthew 13:53–58|MAT 13:53-58\ref*; \ref Luke 4:16–30|LUK 4:16-30\ref*)
\p \v 1 Jesus went on from there and came to His hometown, accompanied by His disciples. \v 2 When the Sabbath came, He began to teach in the synagogue, and many who heard Him were astonished. “Where did this man get these ideas?” they asked. “What is this wisdom He has been given? And how can He perform such miracles? \v 3 Isn’t this the carpenter, the son of Mary and the brother of James, Joses,\f + \fr 6:3 \fqa Joses \ft is a variant of \fqa Joseph\ft ; see \ref Matthew 13:55|MAT 13:55\ref*.\f* Judas, and Simon? Aren’t His sisters here with us as well?” And they took offense at Him.
\p \v 4 Then Jesus said to them, \wj “Only in his hometown, among his relatives, and in his own household is a prophet without honor.”\wj* \v 5 So He could not perform any miracles there, except to lay His hands on a few of the sick and heal them. \v 6 And He was amazed at their unbelief.
\p And He went around from village to village, teaching the people.
\s1 The Ministry of the Twelve
\r (\ref Matthew 10:5–15|MAT 10:5-15\ref*; \ref Luke 9:1–6|LUK 9:1-6\ref*)
\p \v 7 Then Jesus called the Twelve to Him and began to send them out two by two, giving them authority over unclean spirits. \v 8 He instructed them to take nothing but a staff for the journey — no bread, no bag, no money\f + \fr 6:8 \ft Or \fqa copper coins\f* in their belts — \v 9 and to wear sandals, but not a second tunic.
\p \v 10 And He told them, \wj “When you enter a house, stay there until you leave that area. \v 11 If anyone will not welcome you or listen to you, shake the dust off your feet when you leave that place, as a testimony against them.”\wj*\f + \fr 6:11 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa Truly I tell you, it will be more tolerable for Sodom and Gomorrah on the day of judgment than for that town\ft ; see \ref Matthew 10:15|MAT 10:15\ref*.\f*
\p \v 12 So they set out and preached that the people should repent. \v 13 They also drove out many demons and healed many of the sick, anointing them with oil.
\s1 The Beheading of John
\r (\ref Matthew 14:1–12|MAT 14:1-12\ref*; \ref Luke 9:7–9|LUK 9:7-9\ref*)
\p \v 14 Now King Herod heard about this, for Jesus’ name had become well known, and people were saying,\f + \fr 6:14 \ft Literally \fqa they were saying\ft ; some manuscripts \fqa he was saying\f* “John the Baptist has risen from the dead! That is why miraculous powers are at work in him.” \v 15 Others were saying, “He is Elijah,” and still others, “He is a prophet, like one of the prophets of old.”
\p \v 16 But when Herod heard this, he said, “John, whom I beheaded, has risen from the dead!” \v 17 For Herod himself had ordered that John be arrested and bound and imprisoned, on account of his brother Philip’s wife Herodias, whom Herod had married. \v 18 For John had been telling Herod, “It is not lawful for you to have your brother’s wife!”
\p \v 19 So Herodias held a grudge against John and wanted to kill him. But she had been unable, \v 20 because Herod feared John and protected him, knowing that he was a righteous and holy man. When he heard John’s words, he was greatly perplexed; yet he listened to him gladly.
\p \v 21 On Herod’s birthday, her opportunity arose. Herod held a banquet for his nobles and military commanders and the leading men of Galilee. \v 22 When the daughter of Herodias\f + \fr 6:22 \ft Some early manuscripts \fqa When his daughter Herodias\f* came and danced, she pleased Herod and his guests, and the king said to the girl, “Ask me for whatever you wish, and I will give it to you.” \v 23 And he swore to her, “Whatever you ask of me, I will give you, up to half my kingdom!”
\p \v 24 Then she went out and asked her mother, “What should I request?”
\p And her mother answered, “The head of John the Baptist.”
\p \v 25 At once the girl hurried back to the king with her request: “I want you to give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter immediately.”
\p \v 26 The king was consumed with sorrow, but because of his oaths and his guests, he did not want to refuse her. \v 27 So without delay, the king commanded that John’s head be brought in. He sent an executioner, who went and beheaded him in the prison. \v 28 The man brought John’s head on a platter and presented it to the girl, who gave it to her mother.
\p \v 29 When John’s disciples heard about this, they came and took his body and placed it in a tomb.
\s1 The Feeding of the Five Thousand
\r (\ref Matthew 14:13–21|MAT 14:13-21\ref*; \ref Luke 9:10–17|LUK 9:10-17\ref*; \ref John 6:1–15|JHN 6:1-15\ref*)
\p \v 30 Meanwhile, the apostles gathered around Jesus and brought Him news of all they had done and taught. \v 31 And He said to them, \wj “Come with Me privately to a solitary place, and let us rest for a while.”\wj* For many people were coming and going, and they did not even have time to eat.
\p \v 32 So they went away in a boat by themselves to a solitary place. \v 33 But many people saw them leaving and recognized them. They ran together on foot from all the towns and arrived before them. \v 34 When Jesus stepped ashore and saw a large crowd, He had compassion on them, because they were like sheep without a shepherd. And He began to teach them many things.
\p \v 35 By now the hour was already late. So the disciples came to Jesus and said, “This is a desolate place, and the hour is already late. \v 36 Dismiss the crowd so they can go to the surrounding countryside and villages and buy themselves something to eat.”
\p \v 37 But Jesus told them, \wj “You give them something to eat.”\wj*
\p They asked Him, “Should we go out and spend two hundred denarii\f + \fr 6:37 \ft A denarius was customarily a day’s wage for a laborer; see \ref Matthew 20:2|MAT 20:2\ref*.\f* to give all of them bread to eat?”
\p \wj \v 38 “Go and see how many loaves you have,”\wj* He told them.
\p And after checking, they said, “Five — and two fish.”
\p \v 39 Then Jesus directed them to have the people sit in groups on the green grass. \v 40 So they sat down in groups of hundreds and fifties.
\p \v 41 Taking the five loaves and the two fish and looking up to heaven, Jesus spoke a blessing and broke the loaves. Then He gave them to His disciples to set before the people. And He divided the two fish among them all.
\p \v 42 They all ate and were satisfied, \v 43 and the disciples picked up twelve basketfuls of broken pieces of bread and fish. \v 44 And there were five thousand men who had eaten the loaves.
\s1 Jesus Walks on Water
\r (\ref Matthew 14:22–33|MAT 14:22-33\ref*; \ref John 6:16–21|JHN 6:16-21\ref*)
\p \v 45 Immediately Jesus made His disciples get into the boat and go on ahead of Him to Bethsaida, while He dismissed the crowd. \v 46 After bidding them farewell, He went up on the mountain to pray.
\p \v 47 When evening came, the boat was in the middle of the sea, and Jesus was alone on land. \v 48 He could see that the disciples were straining to row, because the wind was against them. About the fourth watch of the night,\f + \fr 6:48 \ft That is, between three and six in the morning\f* Jesus went out to them, walking on the sea. He intended to pass by them, \v 49 but when they saw Him walking on the sea, they cried out, thinking He was a ghost — \v 50 for they all saw Him and were terrified.
\p But Jesus spoke up at once: \wj “Take courage! It is I. Do not be afraid.”\wj* \v 51 Then He climbed into the boat with them, and the wind died down. And the disciples were utterly astounded, \v 52 for they had not understood about the loaves, but their hearts had been hardened.
\s1 Jesus Heals at Gennesaret
\r (\ref Matthew 14:34–36|MAT 14:34-36\ref*)
\p \v 53 When they had crossed over, they landed at Gennesaret and moored the boat. \v 54 As soon as they got out of the boat, the people recognized Jesus \v 55 and ran through that whole region, carrying the sick on mats to wherever they heard He was. \v 56 And wherever He went — villages and towns and countrysides — they laid the sick in the marketplaces and begged Him just to let them touch the fringe of His cloak. And all who touched Him were healed.
\c 7
\s1 The Tradition of the Elders
\r (\ref Matthew 15:1–9|MAT 15:1-9\ref*)
\p \v 1 Then the Pharisees and some of the scribes who had come from Jerusalem gathered around Jesus, \v 2 and they saw some of His disciples eating with hands that were defiled— that is, unwashed.
\p \v 3 Now in holding to the tradition of the elders, the Pharisees and all the Jews do not eat until they wash their hands ceremonially.\f + \fr 7:3 \ft Literally \fqa until they have washed their hands to the fist\f* \v 4 And on returning from the market, they do not eat unless they wash. And there are many other traditions for them to observe, including the washing of cups, pitchers, kettles, and couches for dining.\f + \fr 7:4 \ft NE and WH \fqa cups, pitchers, and kettles.\f*
\p \v 5 So the Pharisees and scribes questioned Jesus: “Why do Your disciples not walk according to the tradition of the elders? Instead, they eat with defiled hands.”
\p \v 6 Jesus answered them, \wj “Isaiah prophesied correctly about you hypocrites, as it is written:\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘These people honor Me with their lips,\wj*
\q2 \wj but their hearts are far from Me.\wj*
\q1 \v 7 \wj They worship Me in vain;\wj*
\q2 \wj they teach as doctrine the precepts of men.’\f + \fr 7:7 \ft \ref Isaiah 29:13|ISA 29:13\ref* (see also LXX)\f*\wj*
\p \v 8 \wj You have disregarded the commandment of God to keep the tradition of men.\f + \fr 7:8 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa —washings of pots and cups and many such things like these.\f*”\wj*
\p \v 9 He went on to say, \wj “You neatly set aside the command of God to maintain\f + \fr 7:9 \ft NA \fqa establish\f* your own tradition. \v 10 For Moses said, ‘Honor your father and your mother’\f + \fr 7:10 \ft \ref Exodus 20:12|EXO 20:12\ref*; Deuteronomy 5:16\f* and ‘Anyone who curses his father or mother must be put to death.’\f + \fr 7:10 \ft \ref Exodus 21:17|EXO 21:17\ref*; Leviticus 20:9\f* \v 11 But you say that if a man says to his father or mother, ‘Whatever you would have received from me is Corban’ (that is, a gift devoted to God ), \v 12 he is no longer permitted to do anything for his father or mother. \v 13 Thus you nullify the word of God by the tradition you have handed down. And you do so in many such matters.”\wj*
\s1 What Defiles a Man
\r (\ref Matthew 15:10–20|MAT 15:10-20\ref*)
\p \v 14 Once again Jesus called the crowd to Him and said, \wj “All of you, listen to Me and understand: \v 15 Nothing that enters a man from the outside can defile him; but the things that come out of a man, these are what defile him.”\wj*\f + \fr 7:15 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa \fv 16\fv*If anyone has ears to hear, let him hear.\f*
\p \v 17 After Jesus had left the crowd and gone into the house, His disciples inquired about the parable.
\p \wj \v 18 “Are you still so dull?”\wj* He asked. \wj “Do you not understand? Nothing that enters a man from the outside can defile him, \v 19 because it does not enter his heart, but it goes into the stomach and then is eliminated.”\wj* (Thus all foods are clean.)\f + \fr 7:19 \ft Or \fqa and then is eliminated, thereby expelling all foods.”\f*
\p \v 20 He continued: \wj “What comes out of a man, that is what defiles him. \v 21 For from within the hearts of men come evil thoughts, sexual immorality, theft, murder, adultery,\f + \fr 7:21 \ft Many texts move \fqa adultery \ft to verse 22.\f* \v 22 greed, wickedness, deceit, debauchery, envy, slander, arrogance, and foolishness. \v 23 All these evils come from within, and these are what defile a man.”\wj*
\s1 The Faith of the Gentile Woman
\r (\ref Matthew 15:21–28|MAT 15:21-28\ref*)
\p \v 24 Jesus left that place and went to the region of Tyre.\f + \fr 7:24 \ft WH, BYZ, and TR include \fqa and Sidon\ft ; see \ref Matthew 15:21|MAT 15:21\ref*.\f* Not wanting anyone to know He was there, He entered a house, but was unable to escape their notice. \v 25 Instead, a woman whose little daughter had an unclean spirit soon heard about Jesus, and she came and fell at His feet. \v 26 Now she was a Greek woman of Syrophoenician origin, and she kept asking Jesus to drive the demon out of her daughter.
\p \wj \v 27 “First let the children have their fill,”\wj* He said. \wj “For it is not right to take the children’s bread and toss it to the dogs.”\wj*
\p \v 28 “Yes, Lord,” she replied, “even the dogs\f + \fr 7:28 \ft Or \fqa puppies\f* under the table eat the children’s crumbs.”
\p \v 29 Then Jesus told her, \wj “Because of this answer, you may go. The demon has left your daughter.”\wj* \v 30 And she went home and found her child lying on the bed, and the demon was gone.
\s1 The Deaf and Mute Man
\r (\ref Matthew 9:27–34|MAT 9:27-34\ref*)
\p \v 31 Then Jesus left the region of Tyre and went through Sidon to the Sea of Galilee and into the region of the Decapolis.\f + \fr 7:31 \ft That is, the Ten Cities\f* \v 32 Some people brought to Him a man who was deaf and hardly able to speak, and they begged Jesus to place His hand on him.
\p \v 33 So Jesus took him aside privately, away from the crowd, and put His fingers into the man’s ears. Then He spit and touched the man’s tongue. \v 34 And looking up to heaven, He sighed deeply and said to him, \wj “Ephphatha!”\wj* (which means, \wj “Be opened!”\wj*). \v 35 Immediately\f + \fr 7:35 \ft ECM, WH, and SBL do not include \fqa Immediately\ft .\f* the man’s ears were opened and his tongue was released, and he began to speak plainly.
\p \v 36 Jesus ordered them not to tell anyone. But the more He ordered them, the more widely they proclaimed it. \v 37 The people were utterly astonished and said, “He has done all things well! He makes even the deaf hear and the mute speak!”
\c 9
\s1 The Transfiguration
\r (\ref Matthew 17:1–13|MAT 17:1-13\ref*; \ref Luke 9:28–36|LUK 9:28-36\ref*; \ref 2 Peter 1:16–21|2PE 1:16-21\ref*)
\p \v 1 Then Jesus said to them, \wj “Truly I tell you, there are some standing here who will not taste death before they see the kingdom of God arrive with power.”\wj*
\p \v 2 After six days Jesus took with Him Peter, James, and John, and led them up a high mountain by themselves. There He was transfigured before them. \v 3 His clothes became radiantly white, brighter than any launderer on earth could bleach them. \v 4 And Elijah and Moses appeared before them, talking with Jesus.
\p \v 5 Peter said to Jesus, “Rabbi, it is good for us to be here. Let us put up three shelters\f + \fr 9:5 \ft Or \fqa three tabernacles\f*— one for You, one for Moses, and one for Elijah.” \v 6 For they were all so terrified that Peter did not know what else to say.
\p \v 7 Then a cloud appeared and enveloped them, and a voice came from the cloud: “This is My beloved Son. Listen to Him!” \v 8 Suddenly, when they looked around, they saw no one with them except Jesus.
\p \v 9 As they were coming down the mountain, Jesus admonished them not to tell anyone what they had seen until the Son of Man had risen from the dead. \v 10 So they kept this matter to themselves, discussing what it meant to rise from the dead. \v 11 And they asked Jesus, “Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?”
\p \v 12 He replied, \wj “Elijah does indeed come first, and he restores all things. Why then is it written that the Son of Man must suffer many things and be rejected? \v 13 But I tell you that Elijah has indeed come, and they have done to him whatever they wished, just as it is written about him.”\wj*
\s1 The Boy with an Evil Spirit
\r (\ref Matthew 17:14–18|MAT 17:14-18\ref*; \ref Luke 9:37–42|LUK 9:37-42\ref*)
\p \v 14 When they returned to the other disciples, they saw a large crowd around them, and scribes arguing with them. \v 15 As soon as all the people saw Jesus, they were filled with awe and ran to greet Him.
\p \wj \v 16 “What are you disputing with them?”\wj* He asked.
\p \v 17 Someone in the crowd replied, “Teacher, I brought You my son, who has a spirit that makes him mute. \v 18 Whenever it seizes him, it throws him to the ground. He foams at the mouth, gnashes his teeth, and becomes rigid.\f + \fr 9:18 \ft Or \fqa and is withering away\f* I asked Your disciples to drive it out, but they were unable.”
\p \wj \v 19 “O unbelieving generation!”\wj* Jesus replied. \wj “How long must I remain with you? How long must I put up with you? Bring the boy to Me.”\wj*
\p \v 20 So they brought him, and seeing Jesus, the spirit immediately threw the boy into a convulsion. He fell to the ground and rolled around, foaming at the mouth.
\p \v 21 Jesus asked the boy’s father, \wj “How long has this been with him?”\wj*
\p “From childhood,” he said. \v 22 “It often throws him into the fire or into the water, trying to kill him. But if You can do anything, have compassion on us and help us.”
\p \wj \v 23 “If You can?”\wj* echoed Jesus. \wj “All things are possible to him who believes!”\wj*
\p \v 24 Immediately the boy’s father cried out,\f + \fr 9:24 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa cried out with tears\f* “I do believe; help my unbelief!”
\p \v 25 When Jesus saw that a crowd had come running, He rebuked the unclean spirit. \wj “You deaf and mute spirit,”\wj* He said, \wj “I command you to come out and never enter him again.”\wj*
\p \v 26 After shrieking and convulsing him violently, the spirit came out. The boy became like a corpse, so that many said, “He is dead.” \v 27 But Jesus took him by the hand and helped him to his feet, and he stood up.
\p \v 28 After Jesus had gone into the house, His disciples asked Him privately, “Why couldn’t we drive it out?”
\p \v 29 Jesus answered, \wj “This kind cannot come out, except by prayer.”\wj*\f + \fr 9:29 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa prayer and fasting\f*
\s1 The Second Prediction of the Passion
\r (\ref Matthew 17:22–23|MAT 17:22-23\ref*; \ref Luke 9:43–45|LUK 9:43-45\ref*)
\p \v 30 Going on from there, they passed through Galilee. But Jesus did not want anyone to know, \v 31 because He was teaching His disciples. He told them, \wj “The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men. They will kill Him, and after three days He will rise.”\wj* \v 32 But they did not understand this statement, and they were afraid to ask Him about it.
\s1 The Greatest in the Kingdom
\r (\ref Matthew 18:1–5|MAT 18:1-5\ref*; \ref Luke 9:46–50|LUK 9:46-50\ref*)
\p \v 33 Then they came to Capernaum. While Jesus was in the house, He asked them, \wj “What were you discussing on the way?”\wj* \v 34 But they were silent, for on the way they had been arguing about which of them was the greatest.
\p \v 35 Sitting down, Jesus called the Twelve and said, \wj “If anyone wants to be first, he must be the last of all and the servant of all.”\wj*
\p \v 36 Then He had a little child stand among them. Taking the child in His arms, He said to them, \wj \v 37 “Whoever welcomes one of these little children in My name welcomes Me, and whoever welcomes Me welcomes not only Me, but the One who sent Me.”\wj*
\p \v 38 John said to Him, “Teacher, we saw someone else driving out demons in Your name, and we tried to stop him, because he does not accompany us.”
\p \wj \v 39 “Do not stop him,”\wj* Jesus replied. \wj “For no one who performs a miracle in My name can turn around and speak evil of Me. \v 40 For whoever is not against us is for us. \v 41 Indeed, if anyone gives you even a cup of water because you bear the name of Christ, truly I tell you, he will never lose his reward.\wj*
\s1 Temptations and Trespasses
\r (\ref Matthew 18:6–9|MAT 18:6-9\ref*; \ref Luke 17:1–4|LUK 17:1-4\ref*)
\p \v 42 \wj But if anyone causes one of these little ones who believe in Me to stumble, it would be better for him to have a large millstone hung around his neck and to be thrown into the sea.\wj*
\p \v 43 \wj If your hand causes you to sin, cut it off. It is better for you to enter life crippled than to have two hands and go into hell,\f + \fr 9:43 \ft Greek \fqa Gehenna\ft ; also in verses 45 and 47\f* into the unquenchable fire.\f + \fr 9:43 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa \fv 44\fv*where their worm never dies, and the fire is never quenched\ft ; see verse 48 and \ref Isaiah 66:24|ISA 66:24\ref*.\f* \v 45 If your foot causes you to sin, cut it off. It is better for you to enter life lame than to have two feet and be thrown into hell.\f + \fr 9:45 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa \fv 46\fv*where their worm never dies, and the fire is never quenched\ft ; see verse 48 and \ref Isaiah 66:24|ISA 66:24\ref*.\f* \v 47 And if your eye causes you to sin, pluck it out. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of God with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hell, \v 48 where  ‘their worm never dies, and the fire is never quenched.’\f + \fr 9:48 \ft \ref Isaiah 66:24|ISA 66:24\ref*\f*\wj*
\s1 Good Salt
\r (\ref Matthew 5:13–16|MAT 5:13-16\ref*; \ref Luke 14:34–35|LUK 14:34-35\ref*)
\p \v 49 \wj For everyone will be salted with fire.\f + \fr 9:49 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa and every sacrifice will be salted with salt\ft .\f*\wj*
\p \v 50 \wj Salt is good, but if the salt loses its saltiness, with what will you season it? Have salt among yourselves, and be at peace with one another.”\wj*
